<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} canonical="https://divorceu.ae/en/divorce-cost-uae/" lang="en" hreflang={hreflang} schema={articleSchema}>
  <Breadcrumb items={breadcrumbs} />

  <!-- Hero -->
  <section class="hero-section">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="overline">Cost Guide · UAE 2026</p>
      <h1>Divorce Cost in UAE 2026: Court Fees, Lawyer Fees &amp; DLD Property Transfer</h1>
      <p class="byline">By Aleksi Suon, UAE Expat &amp; Legal Consultant · Updated October 2026</p>
      <p class="hero-sub">Official court fees for a UAE divorce are low (Dubai Courts charge AED 100 per family application, capped at AED 2,000, and the Abu Dhabi Civil Family Court charges AED 5,000 for a civil divorce), so what you really pay depends on the lawyer, translation, expert reports and, if a home changes hands, Dubai Land Department fees.</p>
      <div class="hero-meta">
        <span>Updated: October 2026</span>
        <span class="sep">·</span>
        <span>Official fees checked against the Dubai fees law, the Abu Dhabi 2022 Regulation and DLD service pages</span>
      </div>
      <div class="hero-cta" style="display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:12px;margin-top:24px;">
        <a href="#contact" style="display:inline-block;padding:13px 28px;background:#c9a84c;color:#fff;border-radius:8px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:700;">Request a Free Consultation</a>
        <a href="https://wa.me/971557344876?text=Hello%2C%20I%20need%20divorce%20legal%20advice%20in%20UAE" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" style="display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:8px;padding:13px 28px;background:#25d366;color:#fff;border-radius:8px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:700;">Chat on WhatsApp</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <main class="main-content">
    <div class="container">

      <!-- Answer-first: official fees by route -->
      <section class="summary-box" id="quick-answer">
        <h2>How Much Does a Divorce Cost in the UAE? Official Court Fees by Route</h2>
        <p class="summary-lead">A UAE divorce costs AED 100 per application in Dubai court fees (capped at AED 2,000) or a flat AED 5,000 at the Abu Dhabi Civil Family Court, plus whatever you agree with a lawyer, plus translation, experts and any property-transfer fees.</p>
        <div class="table-wrapper">
          <table class="cost-table">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Route</th>
                <th>Official court fee</th>
                <th>What to know</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {routeFees.map((row, i) => (
                <tr class={i % 2 === 0 ? '' : 'row-alt'}>
                  <td class="td-label">{row.route}</td>
                  <td class="td-cost">{row.fee}</td>
                  <td class="td-notes">{row.notes}</td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="summary-note-footer">Sources: Dubai Law No. 21 of 2015 on judicial fees (Dubai Legal Portal text), Abu Dhabi Regulation No. 8 of 2022 and the Abu Dhabi Judicial Department court FAQ. Courts can amend fees and show the amount before you pay, so confirm it at registration. Lawyer fees are not set by law and are covered <a href="#lawyer-fees">below</a>.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>How the Full UAE Divorce Bill Adds Up</h2>
        <p>Most cost guides quote one number, usually a lawyer fee, and stop there. Your real bill is a stack of separate items: court fees, lawyer fees, certified translation and legalisation, expert and welfare reports, mediation if you choose it, and Dubai Land Department (DLD) property-transfer fees. The court fees are the smallest part. The property-transfer piece is the one almost nobody explains, and it can dwarf every other cost if a home changes hands at the wrong moment.</p>
        <p>Two things shape the total more than anything else. The first is how much you and your spouse actually agree on: an agreed settlement needs far less lawyer time than a fought case. The second is which law and court apply to you. Muslim divorces run under <strong>Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024</strong> (in force since 15 April 2025, replacing the 2005 Personal Status Law). Non-Muslim residents divorce under <strong>Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2022</strong>, or in Abu Dhabi under <strong>Abu Dhabi Law No. 14 of 2021</strong>, on a no-fault civil track with fewer procedural steps. For the full legal picture, see our <a href="/en/uae-divorce-law/">UAE divorce law overview</a> and the <a href="/en/divorce-in-uae/">complete divorce in UAE guide</a>.</p>
      </section>

      <img
        src="/images/uae-legal-documents.webp"
        alt="UAE legal documents with gold pen, divorce cost guide"
        width="900" height="507"
        loading="lazy" decoding="async"
        class="article-img"
      />

      <!-- Court fees table -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>UAE Court &amp; Ancillary Fees: What You Pay Beyond the Lawyer</h2>
        <p>Government court fees are set by each emirate’s fee legislation and are modest. The ancillary items around them, translation, legalisation, interpreters and expert reports, are where a disputed case gets expensive. The table below lists the official amounts we could verify for Dubai Courts and the Abu Dhabi Judicial Department (ADJD), and marks the items that are priced case by case.</p>

        <div class="table-wrapper">
          <table class="cost-table">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Fee Item</th>
                <th>Amount</th>
                <th>Notes</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {courtFees.map((row, i) => (
                <tr class={i % 2 === 0 ? '' : 'row-alt'}>
                  <td class="td-label">{row.item}</td>
                  <td class="td-cost">{row.cost}</td>
                  <td class="td-notes">{row.notes}</td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="table-note">Dubai figures: Dubai Law No. 21 of 2015 on Judicial Fees of the Dubai Courts. Under the federal Civil Procedure Code, a court fee must be paid within 3 business days of the payment notice or the registration is void (Article 47 of Federal Decree-Law No. 42 of 2022). Dubai also has a committee that can defer or waive fees for people who cannot pay, and victims of domestic violence can be exempted on a request from the Dubai Foundation for Women and Children (Articles 9 and 10 of the Dubai law).</p>
      </section>

      <!-- Lawyer fees: summary and hand-off -->
      <section class="content-section" id="lawyer-fees">
        <h2>Lawyer Fees: The Biggest Cost Variable</h2>
        <p>The lawyer is usually the largest cost in a UAE divorce, and no fee schedule fixes it. The Legal Profession Law sets the ground rules instead: the fee contract must be in writing before the work starts (Article 46 of Federal Decree-Law No. 34 of 2022), fees may be agreed by the type of work or under the firm’s hourly system (Article 48), and the court that heard the case can reduce an agreed fee it finds exaggerated for the effort involved and the benefit to the client (Article 52). Article 47 lists what a fee should reflect:</p>
        <ul class="bullets">
          <li>the type and nature of the work, and the effort and skill it needs;</li>
          <li>the time the work is expected to take;</li>
          <li>the importance of the case or the interests in dispute;</li>
          <li>the lawyer’s experience, standing and seniority, and the firm’s reputation;</li>
          <li>the firm’s own expenses for research and costs.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>In divorce terms: an agreed settlement drafted and filed in one step costs far less than a case with custody, alimony or property claims running through several hearings, an appeal and cassation. For current indicative fee ranges, the fixed, per-hearing and hourly billing models and how to compare quotes, see our guide to <a href="/en/divorce-lawyer-fees-uae/">divorce lawyer fees in the UAE</a>. What the first meeting costs, and when it is free, is covered in the guide to the <a href="/en/divorce-lawyer-consultation-fee-uae/">divorce lawyer consultation fee</a>. If the budget is tight, our page on <a href="/en/divorce-legal-aid-uae/">affordable divorce lawyers and legal aid</a> sets out the cheaper routes.</p>
        <p class="table-note">Location matters for fees and court practice too: compare <a href="/en/divorce-lawyer-dubai/">divorce lawyers in Dubai</a> and <a href="/en/divorce-lawyer-abu-dhabi/">divorce lawyers in Abu Dhabi</a>.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- Abu Dhabi -->
      <section class="content-section" id="abu-dhabi-cost">
        <h2>Abu Dhabi Divorce Cost: Civil Family Court and ADJD Routes</h2>
        <p>In Abu Dhabi the cost depends first on which court hears you. Non-Muslims who qualify use the <strong>Abu Dhabi Civil Family Court</strong> under Abu Dhabi Law No. 14 of 2021 and its 2022 Regulation. Muslims use the ADJD Personal Status Court under Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024.</p>

        <h3 class="subhead">Civil Family Court (non-Muslims)</h3>
        <p>The civil divorce application fee is a fixed <strong>AED 5,000</strong> (Article 13 of the 2022 Regulation). The court grants the divorce at the first hearing once one spouse confirms the wish to separate, with no need to justify it or blame the other spouse (Article 12). That hearing is set at least 30 days after the application is approved (Article 14), and the ADJD says the court aims to finalise a no-fault divorce within one month of registration.</p>
        <p>The cost advantage for expats is language. Court forms and proceedings are bilingual in Arabic and English (Article 17 of the Law), the court can run the whole case in English if the parties do not speak Arabic, and expats are exempt from entering data in Arabic or translating documents from English into Arabic (Article 44 of the Regulation). If a translation or Arabic data entry is needed, the court’s translation section does it for a service fee, so you do not pay a private translator for your English documents. Documents in other languages are a separate cost to budget for.</p>
        <p>Parties may appear in person or appoint a lawyer (Article 45), which is why an agreed civil divorce here is often the most affordable expat divorce in the UAE. The ADJD says applicants must be Abu Dhabi residents or have married at the Civil Family Court, and must come from a country not governed by Sharia law (citizens of Arab League states may be asked for an official document proving religion). It asks for a copy of the marriage certificate and copies of passports and Emirates IDs. Money and children are dealt with separately: a post-divorce application for a financial order follows the judgment, and the court relies on an accounting expert’s report to assess the spouses’ finances (Article 15), which adds expert cost if you cannot agree.</p>

        <h3 class="subhead">ADJD Personal Status Court (Muslims)</h3>
        <p>Muslim divorces in Abu Dhabi go through the ADJD, where the judge may first refer the couple to Family Guidance (Article 8 of Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024). The ADJD Family Guidance Department holds its sessions by video call, and a settlement signed there is approved by the judge and enforceable, which is the cheapest way to close a dispute. On fees, the ADJD says they depend on the case type and are shown before you complete registration, and it lists alimony and family mediation and guidance cases as fee-exempt. Interim personal status orders cost AED 100 (alimony requests are exempt) and a court interpreter costs AED 100 per hour, because Arabic is the official language of these courts. Most hearings are held remotely, which saves time off work.</p>
        <p>For the step-by-step filing process, see <a href="/en/how-to-file-for-divorce-in-abu-dhabi/">how to file for divorce in Abu Dhabi</a>, and for the choice between emirates, <a href="/en/dubai-vs-abu-dhabi-divorce/">Dubai vs Abu Dhabi divorce</a>.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- DLD property transfer -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>Dubai Land Department Property-Transfer Fees</h2>
        <p>If you own a home in Dubai, the largest number in your divorce may not be the lawyer or the court, it is the DLD fee, and it turns on timing. While you are still married, your spouse is a first-degree relative, so a transfer <strong>without compensation</strong> can be registered as a gift at <strong>0.125% of the DLD valuation</strong> (minimum AED 2,000). Once the divorce is final that route closes, and a transfer registered as a sale costs <strong>4% of the value</strong> (the DLD lists 2% for the seller and 2% for the buyer). On a AED 2,000,000 flat, the gift route costs about AED 7,220 in total (AED 2,500 plus about AED 520 in fixed DLD fees plus a AED 4,000 service partner fee and 5% VAT on it), against about AED 84,720 as a sale, a difference of roughly AED 77,500.</p>

        <div class="table-wrapper">
          <table class="cost-table">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Fee Item</th>
                <th>Amount</th>
                <th>Notes</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {dldFees.map((row, i) => (
                <tr class={i % 2 === 0 ? '' : 'row-alt'}>
                  <td class="td-label">{row.item}</td>
                  <td class="td-cost">{row.cost}</td>
                  <td class="td-notes">{row.notes}</td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="table-note">Figures from the DLD “Property Gift Registration” and “Property Sale Registration” service pages, October 2026. The gift fee is calculated on the DLD valuation, not a price you declare, and for land a valuation request must be made at a Real Estate Trustee Center first. There is no automatic 50/50 split of property in the UAE: registered ownership is the starting point, and any claim to a share has to be made and proved. For how this works in practice, see <a href="/en/property-division-divorce-uae/">property division in UAE divorce</a> and <a href="/en/divorce-property-sale-uae/">selling property during a UAE divorce</a>.</p>

        <div class="info-box info-box--warning">
          <h3>Timing Warning</h3>
          <p>The gift route only fits when <strong>both spouses genuinely agree</strong> on who keeps the property, the transfer is made <strong>without payment</strong>, and the paperwork is completed before the divorce is final. If one spouse is buying out the other’s share, that is not a gift, and registering it as one is a risk. If ownership is disputed, do not attempt it: a transfer made to defeat the other party’s claim can be challenged. Get legal advice on your specific case first.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Muslim vs non-Muslim -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>Muslim vs. Non-Muslim: How the Cost Differs</h2>
        <p>The UAE runs a dual family-law system, and which track you fall under changes the process, and the process drives the price.</p>
        <p><strong>Muslim divorce (Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024).</strong> Before a case reaches the court, the supervising judge may refer the couple to Family Guidance if a settlement looks feasible (Article 8). There is no fixed waiting period for this stage, and cases to prove a divorce are exempt. A husband who divorces must document it at court within 15 days, and if he does not without an excuse the court accepts, the wife can claim compensation equal to alimony for the delay (Article 58). In contested harm cases, court-appointed arbitrators have up to 60 days from their appointment to report (the old law allowed 90). Each extra stage adds lawyer time. A wife-initiated <a href="/en/khula-divorce-uae/">khulʿ</a> can be simpler where the husband agrees: she gives an agreed consideration, which may be the dowry she received (Articles 65 and 66); see our page on <a href="/en/mahr-divorce-uae/">mahr in a UAE divorce</a>. Where custody or assets are fought, contested cases sit at the top of the cost range.</p>
        <p><strong>Non-Muslim divorce (Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2022).</strong> Either spouse can ask for a divorce without proving harm or blame (Articles 7 and 8), the case is not referred to Family Guidance, and the court can rule at the first hearing (Article 3). Fewer stages mean fewer hearings and lower lawyer fees for an agreed split. Non-Muslims may instead ask for their home-country law to apply (Article 1), which can add legal work and cost.</p>

        <h3 class="subhead">Dubai vs. Abu Dhabi for Non-Muslims</h3>
        <p>Venue matters. The <strong>Abu Dhabi Civil Family Court</strong> runs bilingual Arabic and English proceedings for a flat AED 5,000 application fee, and expats do not have to translate their English documents into Arabic. In Dubai, the federal civil law is applied by Dubai Courts, whose published family fee schedule is AED 100 per application, capped at AED 2,000 (confirm the fee for a civil case at registration), and proceedings are generally in Arabic, so budget for certified translation of foreign documents. Despite marketing claims, the DIFC and ADGM courts do <strong>not</strong> grant divorces; personal status matters sit with the onshore family courts and the Abu Dhabi Civil Family Court. To compare the filing routes, see <a href="/en/how-to-file-for-divorce-in-dubai/">how to file for divorce in Dubai</a> and <a href="/en/non-muslim-divorce-uae/">non-Muslim divorce in the UAE</a>.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- Worked examples -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>Worked Examples: What a Typical Divorce Actually Costs</h2>
        <p>These examples use only official fees. The lawyer’s fee is shown as a separate line because it depends on your written fee agreement; see <a href="/en/divorce-lawyer-fees-uae/">divorce lawyer fees</a> for indicative ranges.</p>

        <div class="example-card">
          <h3>Example 1: Agreed civil divorce, no property (Abu Dhabi Civil Family Court)</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Civil divorce application fee: <strong>AED 5,000</strong> (Article 13 of the 2022 Regulation)</li>
            <li>Arabic translation of English documents: <strong>not required of you</strong>; the court’s translation section charges a service fee if it translates or enters data (Article 44)</li>
            <li>Lawyer: <strong>optional</strong>, you may appear in person (Article 45); if you instruct one, the agreed fee</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="example-total">Official outlay: <strong>AED 5,000</strong> plus any ADJD translation service fee. The hearing is set at least 30 days after approval.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="example-card">
          <h3>Example 2: Agreed Muslim divorce in Dubai with a property transfer</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Dubai Courts family fees: <strong>AED 100 per application</strong>, capped at AED 2,000</li>
            <li>Certified Arabic translation and legalisation of any foreign documents: <strong>quote-based</strong></li>
            <li>DLD gift registration of a AED 2,000,000 flat to the spouse keeping it, while still married and without payment: <strong>about AED 7,220</strong></li>
            <li>Lawyer to draft or review the settlement: <strong>agreed fee</strong></li>
          </ul>
          <p class="example-total">The same transfer registered as a sale after the divorce would cost <strong>about AED 84,720</strong> in DLD fees, about AED 77,500 more.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="example-card">
          <h3>Example 3: Contested divorce with a custody dispute (Dubai)</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>First-instance family fees: <strong>AED 100 per application</strong>, capped at AED 2,000</li>
            <li>Appeal: <strong>AED 100</strong> (alimony appeals free); cassation: <strong>AED 2,000</strong></li>
            <li>Expert, welfare or valuation reports: <strong>set by the court case by case</strong></li>
            <li>Lawyer through several hearings, an appeal and possibly cassation: <strong>the largest cost by far</strong></li>
          </ul>
          <p class="example-total">The court fees stay small. What makes custody battles the most expensive cases in the UAE system is time: more hearings, expert reports and appeals, each billed by the lawyer.</p>
        </div>
        <p class="table-note">Whether your case is contested or agreed is the biggest single lever on all of the above. See <a href="/en/contested-vs-uncontested-divorce-uae/">contested vs uncontested divorce in the UAE</a> and the <a href="/en/divorce-timeline-uae/">UAE divorce timeline</a> for how duration maps to cost.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- What drives costs -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>What Drives Your Divorce Cost Higher</h2>

        <div class="driver-grid">
          {[
            { factor: 'Dispute over child custody', impact: 'Very High', desc: 'Custody disputes add hearings, social or psychological reports and often an appeal. In personal status matters each side has 30 days to appeal and 30 days for cassation (Article 9 of Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024), and every stage is billed.' },
            { factor: 'Property and asset division', impact: 'High', desc: 'Valuations, accounting expert reports and financial disclosure add cost. In Abu Dhabi civil cases the court relies on an accounting expert report to assess the spouses’ finances (Article 15 of the 2022 Regulation).' },
            { factor: 'Spouse is abroad', impact: 'High', desc: 'Service can be made by SMS, email, smart applications or a recorded call (Article 9 of Federal Decree-Law No. 42 of 2022), which saves cost, but foreign documents still need translation and legalisation, and a plaintiff with no UAE domicile must name a legal representative (Article 44).' },
            { factor: 'Non-Muslim / foreign law cases', impact: 'Medium-High', desc: 'If a spouse asks for home-country law to apply instead of the UAE civil law (Article 1 of Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2022), its content has to be put before the court, which means extra legal work.' },
            { factor: 'Unresponsive spouse', impact: 'Medium', desc: 'The defendant has 10 business days from service to file a defence (Article 48 of the Civil Procedure Code). A spouse who ignores the case slows it down, which adds time more than large cost if a lawyer is managing it.' },
            { factor: 'Translation volume', impact: 'Medium', desc: 'Each foreign document needs certified translation for an Arabic-language court and often legalisation. Large financial disclosure cases (bank records, foreign property documents) accumulate significant translation costs.' },
          ].map(d => (
