            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- How to reduce costs -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>How to Reduce Your Divorce Costs in UAE</h2>

        <div class="tips-list">
          {[
            { tip: 'Reach agreement before filing', detail: 'Every item you agree on outside court, whether the money, alimony, custody schedule or school fees, removes hearings and lawyer time. Draft a settlement first, then have it reviewed and filed.' },
            { tip: 'One lawyer per spouse, but keep it light', detail: 'A lawyer may not help the client’s opponent in the same dispute, even with advice (Article 45 of Federal Decree-Law No. 34 of 2022), so one lawyer cannot act for both of you. In an amicable case, one side’s lawyer can draft and the other spouse can pay for a short review instead of full representation.' },
            { tip: 'Organise your documents before your first meeting', detail: 'Lawyer time spent chasing documents is billed time. Bring your legalised marriage certificate, passports, Emirates IDs, property documents and financial records to your first consultation.' },
            { tip: 'Use Family Guidance well if you are referred', detail: 'A settlement reached at Family Guidance is signed by both of you and the counsellor, approved by the judge, and has the force of an execution writ (Article 8 of Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024). It is the cheapest way to end a Muslim divorce dispute. See our guide to the <a href="/en/family-guidance-section-uae/">Family Guidance Section</a>.' },
            { tip: 'Ask about fee relief', detail: 'Alimony appeals carry no fee in Dubai, a committee can defer or waive court fees for people who cannot pay, and domestic violence victims can be exempted (Dubai Law No. 21 of 2015, Articles 8 to 10). In Abu Dhabi the ADJD lists alimony and family guidance cases as fee-exempt.' },
